.docname {Telemetry Distribution}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Building Status | The state of every room of a building, right now | Science Hall at 14:05 |
    | Latest Value | The newest reading of a metric in a room | Lab 2, 27 °C |
    | Metric Catalog | Everything that can be measured | Temperature, People, Capacity |
    | Focus | The metrics a building compares its rooms on, most important first | Temperature, then People |
    | Reading | One value from one room at one moment | Lab 2, 14:05, 27 °C |
    | Twin | The 3D copy of a real building | The 3D Science Hall |

**Question:** how do people follow a building, live? **Kind:** Core. **Needs:** [Telemetry Ingestion](domain-ingestion.qd), something to follow.

Readings exist. Now each person has to be able to follow them, in a way that fits their work.

---

## In the real world

Sam looks after Science Hall from the facilities office.

- 40 rooms, and Sam needs to spot the odd one out.
- Sam cares about temperature and people, not air quality.
- What Sam knows must always be *now*.

---

## The model

.mermaid
    classDiagram
        class BuildingStatus {
            building
            rooms
        }
        class LatestValue {
            room
            metric
            value
        }
        class Focus {
            building
            metrics
        }
        class CatalogEntry {
            metric
            unit
            owner
        }
        BuildingStatus "1" *-- "many" LatestValue
        Focus "1" o-- "many" CatalogEntry : picks, by importance

Importance is part of the model. What matters most comes first (LD-3).

---

## The catalog is collected, not invented

Each domain describes what it owns. This domain just gathers them.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        T["Telemetry Ingestion: temperature, people, air quality"] --> C["Metric Catalog"]
        B["Building Management: capacity"] --> C
        C --> L["Focus: the building's pick"]

A new metric can be picked as soon as its owner describes it (LD-2).

---

## Two separate things

This is the idea to remember.

| | Building Status | Focus |
|---|---|---|
| Holds | Every metric of every room | Only the chosen metrics |
| Decided by | What really happens | People |
| Used for | Everything that shows the building | Comparing rooms |

**Example.** Ada removes Temperature from the Focus. The 3D twin still colours Lab 2 by temperature, because the status still knows it. The Focus changes what is compared, not what is known.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        R["Reading Recorded"] --> F["Status of Science Hall"]
        F --> T3["3D twin"]
        F --> L{"In the Focus?"}
        L -- yes --> C["Rooms are compared on it"]
        L -- no --> H["Left out of the comparison"]

---

## Rules

1. A reading changes the status of its own building, and no other.
2. Every room of the building can be compared, on its Latest Values (LD-1).
3. A room with no reading has no value, which is not 0. Zero people is a real value.
4. A new building already has a Focus. Nobody has to set one up first (LD-5).
5. Only people whose pass allows the building can follow it.

---

## Staying current

A person must never take an old value for the current one (LD-4).

```text
14:04  Sam stops following. Last value: 25 °C.
14:05  Reading: 27 °C. Sam misses it.
14:07  Sam is back. Sam knows 27 °C.
```

Missed readings do not matter one by one. Only *now* matters here. The past lives in [Telemetry Ingestion](domain-ingestion.qd).

---

## Open questions

- LD-2 and LD-3 say "my columns". One Focus per building, or one per person?
- What is the starting Focus of a new building?
- Does a room in breach need to stand out, or is the value alone enough?
